<?php

/*
 * Copyright (C) 2026 TaKeN.PL Usługi Informatyczne Marek Królikowski
 * Original author: Marek Królikowski (TaKeN)
 * Original project: https://github.com/takenek/galbridge
 * SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
 * See LICENSE and COPYRIGHT for the license and GPL section 7 attribution terms.
 *
 * GAL Bridge (galbridge) - Global Address List for Roundcube - configuration
 *
 * Copy this file to config.inc.php (same directory) or to <roundcube>/config/galbridge.inc.php
 * (read only when the plugin directory has no config.inc.php) and adjust the values. NEVER put a
 * real secret into this .dist file. Your config.inc.php holds the password of the vmail database
 * role: make it readable only by root and the PHP-FPM group, e.g.
 *   chown root:www-data config.inc.php && chmod 0640 config.inc.php
 * (iRedMail on Linux: Roundcube lives in /opt/www/roundcubemail, so this directory is
 * /opt/www/roundcubemail/plugins/galbridge.)
 *
 * Every option shown here has the same default built into the plugin (lib/GalBridge/Config.php),
 * so you only need to set what you want to change. Every galbridge_* option is administrator-only:
 * a stored user preference with such a name is ignored. Numeric limits are clamped to the range
 * given next to them. Entries of domain lists may be Unicode (IDN); they are compared in their
 * lower-case ASCII form.
 *
 * Enable the plugin by adding 'galbridge' to $config['plugins'] in Roundcube's
 * config/config.inc.php (see the last section), then check the installation from the Roundcube
 * directory, as the PHP user (exit code 0 = OK, 2 = problems found):
 *   sudo -u www-data plugins/galbridge/bin/galbridge.sh diag
 *   sudo -u www-data plugins/galbridge/bin/galbridge.sh diag --user=jan.kowalski@example.com
 */

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// VMAIL DATABASE (required)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DSN of the iRedMail vmail database, read with a DEDICATED READ-ONLY role that has column-level
// SELECT grants only - never mailbox.password, recovery_email, allow_nets or settings.
// '' = not configured: no book is offered (fail closed) and diag reports FAIL. There is no fallback
// to Roundcube's own db_dsnw: PostgreSQL cannot query across databases, and iRedMail's "roundcube"
// role (used by the password plugin) can UPDATE vmail.mailbox and read every password hash.
//
// Create the role from the reference grant set. Either print it, with a fresh random password and
// the matching DSN line (stdout only, nothing is written to disk):
//   plugins/galbridge/bin/galbridge.sh grants --driver=mysql [--host=localhost]
//       [--account-host=localhost] [--db=vmail] [--role=galbridge]
//   plugins/galbridge/bin/galbridge.sh grants --driver=pgsql [--host=127.0.0.1]
//       [--db=vmail] [--role=galbridge] [--connection-limit=25]
// (--host = the database server written into the DSN; --account-host = MariaDB/MySQL host part of
// the account, i.e. where Roundcube connects from; README section 2.2), or copy
// SQL/grants.mysql.sql / SQL/grants.postgres.sql out of the plugin directory, edit the copy and run
// it as the database administrator. Re-running a generated output is safe: it (re)sets the
// password, so its DSN line always works - and the previous password stops working. The
// personal-data columns (telephone, mobile, birthday) are a commented-out grant: uncomment it only
// for the fields you enable in galbridge_fields.
//
// Checked before anything connects (a violation disables every book; diag and the log say why):
//  - driver mysql (iRedMail writes "mysqli", accepted as well) or pgsql; the PostgreSQL 'service'
//    parameter (pg_service.conf) is refused, it would hide user and host from these checks;
//  - the role must not be one of galbridge_db_refused_users;
//  - loopback (unix socket, 127.0.0.0/8, ::1, localhost) may be plaintext; ANY other host needs
//    VERIFIED TLS - PostgreSQL sslmode=verify-ca or verify-full (sslmode=require encrypts but does
//    not authenticate the server and is refused), MySQL/MariaDB ca= or capath= together with
//    verify_server_cert=1 - unless galbridge_db_allow_plaintext is set.
// After connecting, a role that can read mailbox.password, recovery_email or allow_nets is refused
// whatever its name (log reason "role_too_broad"): a broad role is never "good enough".
// URL-encode (rawurlencode) a password that contains characters other than A-Z a-z 0-9 - . _ ~
//
// MariaDB / MySQL (iRedMail MySQL edition):
//   TCP on the same host (as iRedMail configures Roundcube itself):
//     'mysql://galbridge:PASSWORD@127.0.0.1/vmail'
//   unix socket (Debian/Ubuntu path; RHEL family: /var/lib/mysql/mysql.sock):
//     'mysql://galbridge:PASSWORD@unix(/run/mysqld/mysqld.sock)/vmail'
//   ('localhost' as the host also makes PDO use its default unix socket.) The host part of the
//   account must match the way you connect: use the one of iRedMail's own roundcube account
//   (SHOW GRANTS FOR 'roundcube'@'localhost';) and pass it to "grants --account-host=...".
//   Remote server with verified TLS (the DSN host name must match the server certificate; the
//   account needs REQUIRE SSL, which "grants" adds for a non-local --account-host):
//     'mysql://galbridge:PASSWORD@db.example.com/vmail?ca=/etc/ssl/certs/vmail-db-ca.pem&verify_server_cert=1'
//     'mysql://galbridge:PASSWORD@db.example.com/vmail?capath=/etc/ssl/certs&verify_server_cert=1'
//   With a client certificate in addition (REQUIRE X509), append
//     &cert=/etc/roundcube/galbridge-client.pem&key=/etc/roundcube/galbridge-client.key
//
// PostgreSQL (iRedMail PostgreSQL edition; its pg_hba.conf accepts md5 password logins over the
// local socket and, through the catch-all line "host all all 0.0.0.0/0 md5", over TCP from any IPv4
// address that listen_addresses lets in):
//   TCP on the same host (as iRedMail configures Roundcube itself):
//     'pgsql://galbridge:PASSWORD@127.0.0.1/vmail'
//   unix socket (give the socket DIRECTORY):
//     'pgsql://galbridge:PASSWORD@unix(/var/run/postgresql)/vmail'
//   Remote server with verified TLS (pg_hba.conf needs "hostssl vmail galbridge 192.0.2.10/32
//   scram-sha-256" and "hostnossl vmail galbridge 0.0.0.0/0 reject" inserted BEFORE iRedMail's
//   catch-all line - pg_hba.conf uses the first matching line, a line after it never applies; give
//   sslrootcert explicitly, the PHP user normally has no ~/.postgresql/root.crt):
//     'pgsql://galbridge:PASSWORD@db.example.com/vmail?sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=/etc/ssl/certs/vmail-db-ca.pem'
//   With a client certificate in addition, append
//     &sslcert=/etc/roundcube/galbridge-client.crt&sslkey=/etc/roundcube/galbridge-client.key
//   Optional parameter: application_name=galbridge (shown in pg_stat_activity).
$config['galbridge_db_dsn'] = '';

// Connect timeout in seconds (1 .. 30), added to the DSN as "timeout" unless the DSN already has
// one. Without it PDO's defaults let a single connection attempt to an unreachable server take
// 30 seconds or more, and every webmail request that touches an address book (opening a message,
// compose, autocomplete) would hang that long. After a failed connection the plugin does not try
// again for 30 seconds in that session (log reason "db_down"). Meanwhile a session keeps listing
// the books it already had, and opening or searching them shows "The directory is temporarily
// unavailable."; a session that has not resolved its books yet (e.g. a new login) gets none until
// the database is back. An outage is never remembered as "no entitlement".
$config['galbridge_db_connect_timeout'] = 2;

// Statement timeout in seconds (1 .. 30), set for the plugin's database session right after
// connecting, together with read-only transaction mode: MariaDB max_statement_time, MySQL
// max_execution_time, PostgreSQL statement_timeout. It bounds what one slow or abusive search can
// cost the database. It is a backstop: the PostgreSQL reference grants also limit the role itself
// (statement_timeout 5s, CONNECTION LIMIT 25 or "grants --connection-limit"); for MariaDB the
// template contains MAX_STATEMENT_TIME and MAX_USER_CONNECTIONS as a commented-out ALTER USER line
// that takes effect only once you uncomment it (SQL/grants.mysql.sql).
$config['galbridge_db_timeout'] = 3;

// true: accept a NON-loopback database host without verified TLS (plaintext, or TLS without
// certificate verification such as sslmode=require). The role password and the directory data
// (names, addresses, lists) then cross the network unprotected, and whoever can intercept or
// redirect the connection (ARP or DNS spoofing) can read them or feed the plugin a forged
// directory. Only for a trusted, isolated network path (e.g. a WireGuard tunnel or a dedicated
// storage VLAN) - prefer TLS. While it is in effect, diag and the log warn.
$config['galbridge_db_allow_plaintext'] = false;

// Database roles never accepted in galbridge_db_dsn (compared case-insensitively), because
// iRedMail gives them far more than GAL Bridge needs: vmail (Postfix, Dovecot, iRedAPD - reads
// every password hash), vmailadmin (iRedAdmin, mlmmjadmin - read-write), roundcube (the password
// plugin - UPDATE on vmail.mailbox), sogo, iredadmin, iredapd, amavisd, fail2ban (other services)
// and the superusers root and postgres. The user of Roundcube's db_dsnw is refused in addition, as
// is the user of password_db_dsn when that option is set in Roundcube's main configuration.
// You may add names. Removing one only weakens this safety net; the column probe above still
// refuses any role that can read password hashes.
$config['galbridge_db_refused_users'] = ['vmail', 'vmailadmin', 'roundcube', 'sogo', 'iredadmin', 'iredapd', 'amavisd', 'fail2ban', 'root', 'postgres'];

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHO IS WHO (IMAP host binding and the viewer cache)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What a user sees is derived from the Roundcube login alone: it is looked up in vmail.mailbox
// (active mailbox in an active, non-backup-MX domain) and the home domain is that row's domain
// column, never the text after '@'. That is safe only when the login was authenticated by an IMAP
// server that uses THIS vmail database. A Roundcube where the IMAP server can be chosen at login
// (a host field, an imap_host array, or a template with %-placeholders such as %d/%n/%s, which
// come from the client's Host header or the login) would let anybody log in through an IMAP
// server of their own as ceo@tenant-a.example and receive tenant A's directory.
//
// Allowed IMAP hosts: bare host names or IP addresses - NO scheme, port or placeholder - e.g.
// ['127.0.0.1'] or ['mail.example.com', 'imap2.example.com']. Both the IMAP host of the session
// and the host stored for the Roundcube user must be in the list.
// [] = derive it from Roundcube's imap_host when that is ONE host without placeholders. iRedMail
// writes 'tls://127.0.0.1:143', which gives '127.0.0.1': nothing to set. With an array or a
// templated imap_host and no explicit list the plugin stays disabled (diag: FAIL), and a malformed
// entry disables it as well (fail closed). List only IMAP servers or Dovecot proxies that
// authenticate against this vmail database. diag checks that the list covers the host of imap_host
// and the hosts stored for the existing Roundcube users; a session outside it is logged as
// host_mismatch with the session host, the stored host and the allowed hosts.
$config['galbridge_imap_hosts'] = [];

// Seconds (30 .. 3600) for which the resolved viewer - home domain, the viewer's own alias
// addresses, the column/privilege probe and therefore the book entitlements - is cached in the
// Roundcube session. Changes in vmail (an account disabled or moved, a new alias, a changed column
// grant) take effect after at most this time. A change of THIS configuration takes effect
// immediately (the cache is bound to a fingerprint of it). Lower = fresher, one lookup more per
// period and session.
$config['galbridge_scope_ttl'] = 300;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DOMAIN ADDRESS BOOK (source id "galbridge_domain")
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The everyday directory: the people, alias accounts and mailing lists of the viewer's own domain
// (or of the viewer's organization, see galbridge_organizations). It has the same per-domain scope
// as iRedMail's LDAP edition (base_dn domainName=%d), so it is safe on a multi-tenant server: no
// tenant ever sees another one through this book.
$config['galbridge_domain_book'] = true;

// Name in the list of address books. '' = the organization label when the viewer's domain belongs
// to one of galbridge_organizations, otherwise the localised "Domain directory".
$config['galbridge_domain_book_name'] = '';

// Viewer domains that get the domain book: ['*'] = every hosted domain (default; '*' is honoured
// only as the sole element), or a list such as ['example.com', 'example.net']. This is an
// allow-list: an entry that cannot be normalised is dropped and reported (ERROR in the log, WARN in
// diag).
$config['galbridge_domain_viewer_domains'] = ['*'];

// Viewer domains that never get the domain book (wins over the list above), e.g. a customer who
// has not agreed to a directory. This is a deny-list: when it is malformed (not an array, an entry
// that cannot be normalised, or '*') the domain book is disabled for EVERYBODY (fail closed, diag
// FAIL) instead of silently showing it to the domains you meant to exclude.
$config['galbridge_domain_viewer_domains_exclude'] = [];

// Groups of the domain book:
//  'auto'       - 'domain' when the viewer's domain belongs to an organization of more than one
//                 domain, else 'department'
//  'department' - one group per value of mailbox.department, compared case-insensitively (needs
//                 'department' in galbridge_fields and its column granted; otherwise the book has
//                 no groups and the log says why)
//  'domain'     - one group per domain of the organization
//  'none'       - no groups
// Groups contain persons only (alias accounts and lists stay single entries), so member counts
// are head counts. Expanding a group (compose to group, a group picked in autocomplete, group
// export) yields all members or - above galbridge_max_group_members - nothing and a visible
// warning (a refused group export downloads no file; a download cannot show a message), never a
// silently truncated recipient list. iRedMail: the open-source iRedAdmin does not
// fill mailbox.department; without values there are simply no department groups.
$config['galbridge_domain_groups'] = 'auto';

// Search-only book: true = listing it without a search shows nothing but Roundcube's "search only"
// notice (as for LDAP directories); entries appear through search and autocomplete only - a
// search-only book has no groups. false (default) = browsable like a personal address book.
// 'auto' = search-only (and without groups) when the book holds more than
// galbridge_searchonly_threshold entries.
$config['galbridge_domain_searchonly'] = false;

// Offer the book in compose autocomplete. The plugin appends the source to Roundcube's
// autocomplete_addressbooks itself (for compose, Redirect-dialog and autocomplete requests only).
// Enforced on the server: with false, an autocomplete request that names this source gets no
// contacts and no groups.
$config['galbridge_domain_autocomplete'] = true;

// Allow exporting the WHOLE book as vCard (Export without a selection or search), up to
// galbridge_max_export entries; false refuses it. A refused export downloads nothing (the server
// answers 204 No Content and logs the reason; an export is a file download and cannot show a
// message). Exporting selected contacts, a search result or a group stays possible within their own
// limits (it shows nothing the user cannot already see on screen).
$config['galbridge_domain_export'] = true;

// Organizations: several domains of ONE customer that share one domain book, e.g.
//   $config['galbridge_organizations'] = [
//       'Acme' => ['acme.pl', 'acme.com', 'acme.de'],
//       'Example Ltd' => ['example.co.uk', 'example.ie'],
//   ];
// A viewer whose domain is listed sees all domains of its organization; the key is the label used
// as the book name (unless galbridge_domain_book_name is set) and as the 'organization' field.
// Domains that are not listed see only themselves. The sets must be disjoint: a domain listed in
// two organizations is removed from both (it falls back to its own domain - fail closed) and diag
// reports FAIL. List primary (mailbox) domains; iRedMail alias domains never hold mailboxes.
// SECURITY: an organization merges directories - never put two different customers into one.
$config['galbridge_organizations'] = [];

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// GLOBAL ADDRESS BOOK (source id "galbridge_global"; cross-domain, OFF by default)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One additional book that shows entries of OTHER domains to selected viewer domains, e.g. for a
// group of companies hosted on one server. It is deliberately stricter than the domain book:
// prefix search only (whatever addressbook_search_mode says); e-mail addresses are matched on the
// local part (or exactly in strict mode), never on a domain fragment, and the 'organization' field
// only by its complete label, so searching for a domain or organization name cannot list a whole
// tenant; EVERY search word must reach galbridge_min_search_length; and a lower result cap
// (galbridge_global_max_results). On a server that hosts unrelated customers
// think twice: this book discloses one customer's people to another.
$config['galbridge_global_book'] = false;

// Name in the list of address books. '' = the localised "Global directory".
$config['galbridge_global_book_name'] = '';

// Viewer domains that get the global book. [] (default) = nobody, so switching the book on alone
// shows nothing; ['*'] = every hosted domain. Allow-list: invalid entries are dropped (ERROR in the
// log, WARN in diag).
$config['galbridge_global_viewer_domains'] = [];

// Viewer domains that never get the global book (deny-list, wins over the list above; malformed =
// the global book is disabled for everybody).
$config['galbridge_global_viewer_domains_exclude'] = [];

// Domains whose entries the global book shows, e.g. ['acme.pl', 'partner.example']. ['*'] = every
// hosted domain, INCLUDING tenants added later - honoured only together with
// galbridge_global_allow_wildcard = true (otherwise the book stays empty and diag warns).
$config['galbridge_global_data_domains'] = [];

// Domains never shown in the global book, also when galbridge_global_data_domains is ['*']
// (deny-list; malformed = the global book is disabled).
$config['galbridge_global_data_domains_exclude'] = [];

// Second, explicit switch for '*' in galbridge_global_data_domains; diag warns while it is in
// effect. Never enable it on a server that hosts unrelated customers.
$config['galbridge_global_allow_wildcard'] = false;

// false (default): the viewer's own domain (or organization) is left out of the global book - it is
// already in the domain book, and every colleague would otherwise appear twice in autocomplete.
// true: show it in the global book as well (e.g. when the domain book is switched off).
$config['galbridge_global_include_home'] = false;

// Groups of the global book: 'none' (default), 'domain' (one group per domain shown) or
// 'department' (needs the department field; equal department names of different domains form ONE
// group). Group expansion follows the same all-or-nothing rule as in the domain book.
$config['galbridge_global_groups'] = 'none';

// Search-only (see galbridge_domain_searchonly). Default true: a cross-domain directory is meant
// to be searched, not browsed.
$config['galbridge_global_searchonly'] = true;

// Offer the global book in compose autocomplete (enforced on the server, as for the domain book).
$config['galbridge_global_autocomplete'] = true;

// Allow exporting the WHOLE global book as vCard. Default false: a bulk export of other domains'
// people is refused; selected contacts, search results and groups stay exportable.
$config['galbridge_global_export'] = false;

// Entries (persons + alias accounts + lists) above which a book with searchonly = 'auto' becomes
// search-only. Applies to both books.
$config['galbridge_searchonly_threshold'] = 2000;   // 1 .. 1000000

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CONTENT (fields, and which entries are listed)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Field allowlist (data minimisation). A field that is not listed is never shown, searched, sorted
// on, grouped on or exported. Available fields:
//   'name'          display name: mailbox.name, else first + last name, else the local part (always on)
//   'email'         the mailbox address, plus alias addresses with galbridge_user_aliases (always on)
//   'firstname'     mailbox.first_name           'surname'   mailbox.last_name
//   'department'    mailbox.department
//   'phone'         mailbox.telephone (work)     'mobile'    mailbox.mobile
//   'organization'  the label from galbridge_organizations (not read from vmail; in the global book
//                   a search word matches it only when it equals the whole label)
//   'birthday'      mailbox.birthday (never searchable)
// The effective set is this list minus the columns the database role cannot read: grant the
// optional columns for exactly the fields you enable (SQL/grants.*.sql grant department,
// first_name and last_name; telephone, mobile and birthday are a commented-out statement). A field
// whose column is missing or not granted is dropped with a warning instead of breaking the book,
// and diag lists granted columns that no configured field uses (INFO). iRedMail:
// first_name, last_name, telephone, mobile and birthday exist since iRedMail 1.7.3, and the
// open-source iRedAdmin fills only mailbox.name - so set addressbook_sort_col = 'name' (last section).
// PRIVACY: phone, mobile and birthday are personal data shown to every viewer of the book; enable
// them only with a legal basis (e.g. GDPR art. 6) and an internal policy.
$config['galbridge_fields'] = ['name', 'firstname', 'surname', 'email', 'department'];

// Per-user alias addresses (iRedMail forwardings rows with is_alias = 1, e.g. jan@example.com for
// jan.kowalski@example.com): true = shown as an additional ("other") address of their owner and
// matched by searches. Default false: they are neither shown nor searchable, so a search for an
// alias cannot reveal who receives it (e.g. which person is behind ceo@ or abuse@). The viewer's
// OWN aliases are always used internally to decide which restricted lists the viewer may see.
$config['galbridge_user_aliases'] = false;

// iRedMail alias accounts (table "alias": an address that forwards to its members, "Mail alias"
// in iRedAdmin) are listed as single entries, subject to galbridge_list_policy.
$config['galbridge_include_aliases'] = true;

// mlmmj mailing lists (table "maillists") are listed as single entries, subject to
// galbridge_list_policy. Their subscribers are kept by mlmmj on disk, not in SQL, and never shown.
$config['galbridge_include_mailing_lists'] = true;

// Who sees alias accounts and mailing lists, following their iRedAPD posting policy (the
// accesspolicy column):
//  'strict'      - only viewers who may post to them (default): public or empty = everybody in the
//                  book; domain = viewers of the entry's domain; subdomain = viewers of that
//                  domain and its sub-domains; membersonly, moderatorsonly and
//                  membersandmoderatorsonly = members, moderators or owners among the viewer's
//                  addresses (mlmmj lists: moderators and owners only, subscribers are not in SQL).
//                  An unknown policy hides the entry (fail closed).
//  'public_only' - only entries with a public (or empty) policy
//  'all'         - every active entry whatever its policy. This reveals restricted lists (e.g.
//                  board@ or hr-cases@) to people who cannot even write to them: explicit opt-in.
$config['galbridge_list_policy'] = 'strict';

// Hide mailboxes, alias accounts, lists and domains whose iRedMail expiry time (the "expired"
// column; 9999-12-31 means never) has passed, compared with the current UTC time. false = ignore
// expiry (the expired columns are then not needed).
$config['galbridge_hide_expired'] = true;

// Addresses that are never listed or matched in any book (persons, alias accounts and lists), e.g.
// ['ceo@example.com', 'backup-robot@example.com']. Deny-list: a malformed list disables ALL books
// (fail closed, diag FAIL).
$config['galbridge_hidden_addresses'] = [];

// Local parts hidden in EVERY domain, e.g. ['postmaster', 'abuse', 'root', 'noreply'] (iRedMail
// creates postmaster@ of the first domain as a real mailbox). Deny-list: malformed = all books
// disabled.
$config['galbridge_hidden_localparts'] = [];

// Name of an optional, administrator-created column of vmail.mailbox that decides per person
// whether they are listed (1 = listed, any other value = hidden). '' = no such column (default).
// The column is not part of the iRedMail schema and iRedAdmin does not know it: create it with a
// default, so that mailboxes added later get a defined value, and grant it to the role, e.g.
//   MariaDB:    ALTER TABLE vmail.mailbox ADD COLUMN galbridge_visible TINYINT(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 1;
//               GRANT SELECT (galbridge_visible) ON vmail.mailbox TO 'galbridge'@'localhost';
//   PostgreSQL: ALTER TABLE mailbox ADD COLUMN galbridge_visible INT2 NOT NULL DEFAULT 1;
//               GRANT SELECT (galbridge_visible) ON mailbox TO galbridge;
// (an integer column, not a PostgreSQL BOOLEAN). A configured column that the role cannot read
// lists no person at all (fail closed); an invalid name (lower-case letters, digits and '_', not
// starting with a digit, at most 64 characters) disables all books. It applies to persons only;
// hide alias accounts and lists with galbridge_hidden_addresses.
$config['galbridge_visibility_column'] = '';

// In the global book, entries from outside the viewer's own domain (or organization) are shown as
// "Name (domain)", so that a look-alike name in another tenant is not mistaken for a colleague.
// Such entries carry no first and last name, so every "List names as" mode of Roundcube
// (addressbook_name_listing) shows the marked name. The marker is part of the display name: it also
// appears in recipient fields, exported vCards and contacts copied to a personal address book.
$config['galbridge_mark_foreign_domains'] = true;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// LIMITS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Minimum search word length (one value for all books). No query runs unless at least one word
// reaches it (global book: EVERY word); shorter words only narrow as word prefixes, so "Jan K"
// works. At most 5 words are used. In compose and in the Redirect dialog the plugin raises
// Roundcube's autocomplete_min_length to this value, because Roundcube's JavaScript stops asking
// the server once a prefix returned nothing.
$config['galbridge_min_search_length'] = 2;         // characters (1 .. 10)
$config['galbridge_max_search_length'] = 128;       // characters (16 .. 255); longer input matches nothing

// Caps of search result lists. A longer result is cut with the notice "Only the first N directory
// matches are shown. Refine your search."
$config['galbridge_max_results'] = 500;             // domain book (10 .. 5000)
$config['galbridge_global_max_results'] = 100;      // global book (10 .. 5000)

// Compose to selected contacts and export of selected contacts: more selected directory contacts
// than this are refused - never silently cut to a prefix (compose shows a message; a refused export
// downloads nothing).
$config['galbridge_max_ids'] = 200;                 // 1 .. 1000

// Members of one group that can be expanded at once: compose to group, a group picked in
// autocomplete, group export. A larger group is refused with a message that suggests a mailing
// list - never silently cut. The effective cap is the smaller of this value and Roundcube's
// max_group_members (when that is greater than 0); diag shows it.
$config['galbridge_max_group_members'] = 500;       // 1 .. 100000

$config['galbridge_max_groups'] = 50;               // groups per book (1 .. 500); above it the book shows no groups
$config['galbridge_max_export'] = 2000;             // entries of a whole-book vCard export (1 .. 100000); above it refused
$config['galbridge_max_count'] = 10000;             // upper bound of counts (100 .. 1000000); list pages beyond it are empty

// There is deliberately NO per-session request rate limiter (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, section 11): a
// counter in the Roundcube session is not atomic - parallel autocomplete requests all pass - and
// would turn read-only requests into session writers. Resource control is: the caps above, the
// minimum search length, the connect and statement timeouts, the role's connection limit
// (SQL/grants.*.sql) and - recommended - a rate limit on _action=autocomplete in the reverse proxy
// (README).

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// LOGGING
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Log channel "galbridge", written through Roundcube's log driver (the file
// <log_dir>/galbridge.log - Roundcube appends log_file_ext, default '.log' - or syslog: iRedMail
// sets log_driver 'syslog' with facility LOG_MAIL). Normal logging holds reason codes
// (denied_source, invalid_id, group_too_large, role_too_broad, db_error with the SQLSTATE, ...),
// the numeric Roundcube user id, book ids and counts - no search terms, names, addresses or
// passwords. Reason codes triggered by request input or by directory rows are logged at most once
// per request; db_error is logged for every failed query.
// true adds only the database driver's error text, the messages of unexpected exceptions and
// schema hints. The plugin never logs search terms or addresses itself, but those texts are not
// filtered for personal data (a driver error may quote part of a statement with the inlined search
// values) - enable it only while troubleshooting. Independently of this option
// the DATABASE server's own logs (slow/general query log, PostgreSQL log_min_error_statement or
// log_min_duration_statement) can contain search terms, because rcube_db inlines bound values
// (SECURITY.md).
$config['galbridge_debug'] = false;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RECOMMENDED ROUNDCUBE CORE SETTINGS (comments only)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Set these in Roundcube's own config/config.inc.php (iRedMail:
// /opt/www/roundcubemail/config/config.inc.php), NOT in this file.
//
// Enable the plugin (iRedMail ships array('managesieve', 'password', 'zipdownload')):
//   $config['plugins'] = ['managesieve', 'password', 'zipdownload', 'galbridge'];
//
// Sort contacts by display name. iRedMail keeps Roundcube's default 'surname', but the open-source
// iRedAdmin fills only mailbox.name, and Roundcube re-sorts search results by this column in PHP:
// with 'surname' most directory entries end up sorted by e-mail address. diag warns about any
// other value. Users can change it in Settings > Preferences > Contacts unless it is locked:
//   $config['addressbook_sort_col'] = 'name';
//   $config['dont_override'] = ['addressbook_sort_col'];      // optional; merge with your list
//
// Autocomplete. In compose and in the Redirect dialog the plugin raises autocomplete_min_length to
// galbridge_min_search_length and appends its enabled books to autocomplete_addressbooks by itself
// (also for the autocomplete requests themselves). Set them explicitly only to
// make the values visible or to fix the order of the sources (duplicates are removed):
//   $config['autocomplete_min_length'] = 2;                    // >= galbridge_min_search_length
//   $config['autocomplete_addressbooks'] = ['sql', 'galbridge_domain', 'galbridge_global'];
//   $config['autocomplete_max'] = 15;                          // entries in the popup (default 15)
//   $config['autocomplete_threads'] = 0;                       // 0 (default) = all sources in one
//                                                              // request; n > 0 = parallel requests,
//                                                              // count them in a proxy rate limit
//   $config['autocomplete_single'] = false;                    // true = one address per contact
//
// Group expansion: Roundcube's own limit (default 0 = none) also caps directory groups - the
// smaller of it and galbridge_max_group_members wins:
//   $config['max_group_members'] = 0;
//
// Matching mode of the domain book (the global book always uses prefix search):
//   $config['addressbook_search_mode'] = 0;                    // 0 partial (default), 1 strict, 2 prefix
//
// Do not run the old sql_global_addressbooks plugin at the same time, and remove its _sql_* options;
// diag reports leftovers (docs/MIGRATION.md).
